WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.159 --> 00:00:08.992
- I am now delighted

00:00:08.992 --> 00:00:13.290
to introduce our speaker Daniel Alvarenga.

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.770
Daniel is a Salvadorian
American journalist

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:17.630
based in Washington, DC.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:19.390
Is a writer for Telemundo's

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:23.140
English language new show, Radar2020.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.200
He formerly worked as a producer for AJ+,

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.370
a digital channel by Al Jazeera.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:32.430
He regularly reports on
politics, social movements,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.580
and climate justice in Central America

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.310
and the United States.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.790
He's the son of refugees
who fled the civil war

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:41.640
in El Salvador in the 1980s.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.970
He studied linguistics at
the University of California,

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:48.410
Santa Barbara for undergrad
and received a Masters

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:53.260
in Corporate Communications
from IE University in Madrid.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.250
I cannot tell you how special it is for me

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.850
to personally present Daniel to you.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:00.940
For many years I did not
see the stories of my family

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.940
as Salvadorian refugees of
people from Central America

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.210
then yells work seeks to
unapologetically center,

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:11.740
the voices histories,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.160
and experiences for people of the isthmus,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.630
and I am personally thankful to him

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.340
that my nieces and nephew
won't have to experience

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.690
the relation that so many
of us have experienced.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.240
With that, please help me
welcome Daniel Alvarenga.

00:01:27.464 --> 00:01:30.160
- Hi, thank you everyone,
I'm Daniel Alvarenga

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.810
here from Washington DC.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.690
I'm originally from the
L.A metropolitan area

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:37.520
which is home to the largest amount

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:40.410
of Central American diaspora
outside of El Salvador.

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.830
And I'm in DC where it has
like the highest per capita,

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.265
It's the biggest immigrant group.

00:01:44.265 --> 00:01:49.265
And yes, I'm a journalist like
I was introduced by Eileen.

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:54.070
I originally, when I signed on to this,

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.890
I was still working Al Jazeera AJ+,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.800
I spent the last three years there.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.050
So much of this talk is gonna
be focus on my work there

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.370
but I'm also now with
Telemundo for a new show.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.280
And I'll talk about that
a little bit at the end,

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:10.973
but I'm kind of we're
gonna walk you through

00:02:10.973 --> 00:02:14.180
some of the stories that I've worked on.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:17.520
Some of the themes that I touch,
some of my favorite stories

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.230
and what's shaped my storytelling

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:24.750
and my analysis on Central Americans

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.403
in the U.S and globally.

00:02:29.504 --> 00:02:32.850
And like Eileen said, part of
the reason I got into this,

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:37.850
was because I was
frustrated at the coverage.

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.150
I was a student journalist
like 10 years ago

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.480
then I couldn't get a job at the industry.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.870
And then I just started
tweeting into the void

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.500
and started writing and started blogging

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.980
and then sort of built a
journalism career starting 2014

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.370
with the migrant crisis
that happened that year

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.113
that centered Central Americans.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:02.060
Before we get into it, I need to talk

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.523
about who are Central Americans.

00:03:05.789 --> 00:03:08.230
Who are Central Americans?

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:10.893
Central America is seven countries.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.500
It is multiethnic, multiracial,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.500
and it's not just Mestizo in European,

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.320
it's home to a lot of indigenous folks,

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.933
it's home to Afro indigenous folks.

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:29.180
They don't just speak Spanish, I'm there,

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:33.100
parts speak English and
believes in certain coasts,

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.523
Creole languages and a lot
of indigenous languages.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.150
It's a mega diverse region and it's hard

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.143
to really encapsulate it, I think,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.440
but I'll try to make sort of footnotes

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:51.760
on the other Central American countries

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:56.270
but my reporting is gonna focus mostly

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.220
on the top three right here,

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.230
Northern top three, Guatemala,
Honduras, El Salvador.

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.258
And I'll get into sort of why that is.

00:04:04.258 --> 00:04:06.740
And that's sort of been the
bulk of my work as well,

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.570
those three countries.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:12.470
So here we have a look at the
Central American population

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.210
from 1980 - 2015, this is from a piece

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.080
about how U.S involvement
in Central America

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.130
led to the border crisis.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:23.053
It's by AJ+, I helped
produce it, is on YouTube.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:28.720
Basically there wasn't a whole
lot of us compared to today.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.280
In 1980, there was like
less than a million.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.690
Now there's over three
million in three decades.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:40.003
And what happened in
1980 to now, civil war,

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:46.010
neoliberal economic policies
in the post-war period

00:04:46.010 --> 00:04:49.453
of El Salvador gang violence,
a ton of U.S intervention.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:55.180
And this is a population
for all of Central Americans

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.420
in the U.S but it's
largely made up of people

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:04.420
from the Northern Triangle
and the Northern Triangle,

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:08.350
I never really questioned
that term before.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.140
When I came into college,
it was already being used.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:14.340
I just thought it was like
sort of a formal place,

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.290
a formal way we referred to this place.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:19.980
But what was the Northern Triangle?

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.620
The Northern Triangle was actually,

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.850
it started off as not really meaning much

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:28.030
in terms of nobody's to
say Northern Triangle

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:29.680
in the nineties, in the eighties,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:32.630
but it was a commercial designation.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.180
It was a commercial designation

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.060
and it usually represents free trade,

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.120
neoliberal policies in the region.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.820
So it started in 1982,
it gained popularity

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:43.803
with the free trade agreement, Mexico,

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:50.320
and it's, okay, it sounds
harmless if you think

00:05:50.848 --> 00:05:52.700
neoliberal policies are having this,

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:57.370
but it sounds harmless
this sort of designation,

00:05:57.370 --> 00:05:59.680
but it's sort of taken on a different life

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.203
since this commercial designation.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.130
So now the Northern
Triangle is just synonymous

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.290
with death, violence.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:13.830
Here are a couple of
headlines, I'll translate them

00:06:13.830 --> 00:06:16.030
for those who don't speak Spanish,

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:18.320
Spanish section of the Chicago Tribune

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.457
called it, "The Northern Triangle,

00:06:21.457 --> 00:06:24.913
"What Are The Most Violent
Zones In The World?"

00:06:24.913 --> 00:06:29.913
El Mundo called it,
"The Triangle Of Death,

00:06:30.047 --> 00:06:33.320
The Invisible Routes Of Refugees."

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.357
And then, Sky News has it, if
you can't read it's too small,

00:06:37.357 --> 00:06:41.480
"Northern Triangle, The
Vicious Gave War Being Fought

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:42.790
Over Every Street."

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:45.737
And then this sort of
hopeful one from 2018,

00:06:45.737 --> 00:06:49.040
"The Northern Triangle Is
Becoming Less Murderous."

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:49.873
But the implication

00:06:49.873 --> 00:06:52.030
is that the Northern
Triangle was murderous.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:57.750
And it's through this lens

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.400
that I came to know the Northern Triangle.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.420
I think most of us know
the Northern Triangle,

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:05.750
it came from a commercial designation

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:07.403
and it became this narrative.

00:07:12.220 --> 00:07:15.790
The Northern Triangle
is also the countries

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.253
that are coming here the most.

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:22.780
As you can see, this is data
from the Pew Research Center.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.173
You can find that at the
Pew Research website,

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.731
going into the Trump administration

00:07:30.731 --> 00:07:32.710
the last years of the Obama years,

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:36.940
you saw a ton of migration
Northern Triangle

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:40.190
to the point where like
the Northern Triangle

00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:41.840
sort of lumped in with itself,

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:44.690
here and kind of counted as one entity

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:49.690
and it started out pacing immigration

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.870
from Mexico, which has historically been,

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.140
one of the more constant
immigration waves in the country.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:04.013
And around 2014, you
started to see a shift.

00:08:04.013 --> 00:08:07.830
As a Salvador, I always constantly grew up

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:11.270
meeting people coming from El Salvador,

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:12.530
they're coming through L.A,

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:15.100
they're gonna go to
their next destination.

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:16.670
L.A was so close to the border

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:18.680
relatively than here in Washington DC,

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:23.100
and I don't remember a time growing up.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:26.240
I was born in 1989, the
tailoring of the war.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:31.240
I don't recall a time where
people weren't coming to the U.S

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:35.060
from El Salvador, and
that's also can be said

00:08:35.060 --> 00:08:37.183
for Guatemala and Honduras as well.

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:43.560
If anything, the more recent
waves are heavily favored

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:46.343
Honduras and Guatemala versus El Salvador.

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.430
We'll get into some of that.

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:57.180
So then this happened
in the summer of 2018.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:00.943
I was a year into my job at Al-Jazeera.

00:09:02.010 --> 00:09:05.360
I didn't really produce news videos.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:08.890
I wasn't creating a whole lot for them.

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:12.000
I was a freelancer but I was
sort of updating their Twitter.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.760
And so I would go through
all the breaking news

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.710
all the time and I was
constantly pitching stories

00:09:18.710 --> 00:09:19.580
about Central America, it's like,

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:22.370
we need to talk about Central America.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:24.070
And everyone thought
like, okay yeah, Daniel,

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:26.130
you're from there, it's a niche for you.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:27.800
You're passionate about it.

00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:31.450
We hear you, but there's
not a whole lot of audience

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:35.220
for the elections in Honduras

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.340
for what's going on in El Salvador.

00:09:37.340 --> 00:09:38.863
And then this happens,

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:45.690
reports started coming out in mid 2018

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:50.690
that 200 children were
separated at the border,

00:09:51.347 --> 00:09:54.270
at the U.S Mexican border.

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.300
Around 2000 miners were
separated from their family.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:59.380
And we're still seeing some
of this play out today.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:02.850
They're still like, they couldn't find 500

00:10:04.636 --> 00:10:06.370
of these unifications, like 500 of them

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.870
are still pending and they
can't find the parents.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:13.050
Some of these were younger
than three years old.

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:16.920
It captivated the country,
these headlines were everywhere

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:19.940
and these images were everywhere.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:23.060
I was becoming a journalist at this point.

00:10:23.060 --> 00:10:26.243
And I was showing all of
my clips to my family.

00:10:27.329 --> 00:10:31.040
All of my family is, I'm the
only one who's not a refugee.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.760
My siblings are refugees
from the civil war.

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:36.360
My parents were refugees
from the civil war.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:37.780
So I always share my stories with them.

00:10:37.780 --> 00:10:40.650
And I think at this
point is when I started

00:10:40.650 --> 00:10:42.704
just not sharing them as much

00:10:42.704 --> 00:10:44.280
as being a little bit more selective

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:48.860
because it was overwhelming for all of us

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:50.750
to just be bombarded with this news.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.900
And it wasn't new to them,
they were listening to it

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:57.900
on the TV as well but I'll never forget

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:00.896
that moment in the newsroom that it took

00:11:00.896 --> 00:11:04.480
the theft of thousands of
children from Central America

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:11.030
for me to start giving editorial opinions

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:15.200
because I was kind of the only expert

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.100
in the newsroom at the
time that really understood

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:21.350
intimately Central America
and had a connection to it.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:24.300
So I started taking on
a lot of these stories

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:30.530
and there was also the ProPublica thing

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:33.440
was also another like watershed
moment around the same time,

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:38.300
same year when the children
were separated at the border,

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.130
And there was recording,

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:42.571
that was like the evidence, the proof,

00:11:42.571 --> 00:11:45.427
and that was broke by ProPublica.

00:11:45.427 --> 00:11:48.440
And we did a video with some of the audio,

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:49.580
the border agent goes, where are you from,

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:52.800
the child says El Salvador and you,

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.630
another child says Guatemala

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:57.080
and then you hear a
cacophony of children crying.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:11:59.520
And this also captivated the nation.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:00.560
And I was just kinda like,

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.110
whoa, maybe people are
finally starting to listen

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:08.850
to Central Americans or tune in

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:11.763
to Central American politics and issues.

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:14.400
But one thing I've learned in news

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.110
and just living life
that the attention span

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:20.070
for the news cycle for
anything really is only so big,

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:25.070
and for Central Americans,
the amnesia is pretty bad.

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:29.230
So these images, we all saw these images

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:31.183
plastered everywhere two years ago.

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:34.090
I know it's not interactive

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:35.950
but I'm gonna give you a second to guess

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:38.463
what year do you think this is from.

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:46.430
Since this is the stock image
that you see with every story.

00:12:46.430 --> 00:12:47.480
So when I'm looking at a story

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:51.850
up on Reuters, Associated
Press, I see this all the time.

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:53.800
And these are kind of the images

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.950
that a lot of articles
will use a stock images

00:12:57.950 --> 00:12:58.970
for their articles.

00:12:58.970 --> 00:13:02.113
But they are not from
the Trump administration.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:06.050
These images though, I'm
sure they're still relevant

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.000
to what's going on,

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:10.420
they're not from the Trump administration.

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:12.073
They're actually from 2014.

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:19.420
It got so bad in 2018 that
the AP had to fact check

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:20.893
with its own photos.

00:13:23.120 --> 00:13:26.453
They are being used to accuse
the Trump government of,

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:29.290
being used as proof of Trump's policies.

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:32.270
When in fact, they're actually the truth

00:13:33.511 --> 00:13:37.030
of the Obama era policy
that builds upon the legacy

00:13:37.030 --> 00:13:39.583
of George W. Bush and Reagan and Clinton.

00:13:40.710 --> 00:13:44.640
So I always want to impart
that sort of this immigration

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:47.930
moment that we were in, wasn't
just brought about by Trump,

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:51.623
It's been a bipartisan strategy for years.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:56.950
Yeah, and these images still get used.

00:13:56.950 --> 00:14:00.410
They're still pretty relevant
to a lot of the news going on,

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:05.410
and there's not a lot of stock
images from ICE facilities.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:08.410
So I don't blame journalists
for having to use this

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:12.390
because there's not a whole lot for us to,

00:14:12.390 --> 00:14:15.910
there's not a lot of people
going inside of these attentions

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:20.883
all the time or you get like
these doctored PR photos often,

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:25.250
especially in the last year or two.

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:29.313
2019 I started seeing a lot
more propaganda style photos.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:34.970
But it just shows that we
just have such an amnesia

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:40.300
with Central American immigration

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:44.160
and sort of the U.S has
relation to that region

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:46.640
because just six years ago,

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:50.620
the 2014 Central American
unaccompanied minor crisis

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:54.453
when 70,000 unaccompanied
minors came to the border,

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:00.840
these kids, they were like
kinda young to like teens,

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:04.400
some of these kids,
they're not kids anymore.

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:05.233
This is six years ago.

00:15:05.233 --> 00:15:07.350
They're in college classes.

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:09.813
They message me on
Twitter asking me things.

00:15:11.350 --> 00:15:16.350
They're part of the population
here in the U.S now,

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:19.223
and we've forgotten about them,

00:15:23.948 --> 00:15:27.170
And we forgot this happened in 2018.

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:32.170
And I would say 2014 was like
my journalism origin story,

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:34.910
because I was so heated at the fact

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:39.210
that a lot of people are
like, how did this happen?

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:40.550
How did we get here?

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:45.550
And knowing me, I never remember
a period where Salvadorans

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:49.190
didn't come here all the time

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:51.830
and by extension other Central Americans.

00:15:51.830 --> 00:15:55.360
So to me it was very weird
that we were acting 2014

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:56.690
like this was a new thing.

00:15:56.690 --> 00:16:00.230
And then again, in 2018, we
acted like it was a new thing.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:05.230
And so I always push
people in the organizations

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:11.080
that I'm a part of, to
really think a little deeper

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:15.680
about the U.S relationship
with Central America.

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:17.250
It's only like two or
three countries away.

00:16:17.250 --> 00:16:18.530
I know Mexico is massive

00:16:21.739 --> 00:16:24.750
but there was another media moment.

00:16:24.750 --> 00:16:27.010
There was several media moments

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:30.550
and one of them, and
there were several deaths

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:33.290
on the border that had
happened around this point.

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:36.770
But I think what really galvanized
people was that children

00:16:36.770 --> 00:16:40.940
were dying on the border in ICE custody.

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:44.030
We have different ones here
since fourth, fifth, sixth.

00:16:44.030 --> 00:16:46.343
There was, I think up to seven children.

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:52.280
There's been probably hundreds of adults

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:53.730
that have died in ICE custody

00:16:56.857 --> 00:16:58.830
since the agency was open in 2013,

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:00.353
you're gonna look at the
records, it's not just Trump,

00:17:00.353 --> 00:17:02.470
It's not even just the Obama era,

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:06.443
though a lot of it ramped up
in these last administrations.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.950
So these children were dying,

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:14.350
and again, it was like, Whoa
America is starting to listen.

00:17:14.350 --> 00:17:15.883
U.S is starting to listen.

00:17:18.820 --> 00:17:23.820
They became symbols,
Jakelin Caal, Felipe Alonzo.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:28.510
So Jakelin Caal, this image here

00:17:28.510 --> 00:17:31.640
is by visual artists on brooms.

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:36.640
And this image was in so many
protests across the country.

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:39.190
So many people printed it out.

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:43.453
I saw it at protest against ICE.

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:46.450
These children have become a symbol.

00:17:46.450 --> 00:17:49.800
Here is Zoe Saldana sharing Felipe Alonzo,

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:53.060
Another one of the children
who died of neglect

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:56.420
at the border, and then
Don Cheadle's photo,

00:17:56.420 --> 00:17:58.800
Don Cheadle the Hollywood actor,

00:17:58.800 --> 00:18:03.280
his Twitter photo to this
day is still Jakelin Caal,

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:10.870
Indigenous Maya girl who
was killed at the border

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:14.943
or died of neglect,
however you wanna say it.

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:21.750
But Jakelin Caal's name
kind of was like that to me.

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:24.070
'Cause I know the region, it didn't sound

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:25.810
like a Hispanic last name,

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:30.030
it sounded like perhaps
a MileIQ last name,

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:33.930
so I did the story with
the international MileIQ

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:35.650
about these children,

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:39.990
and this is a quote from Juanita Lopez

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:43.127
of the international
MileIQ, and I read it out.

00:18:43.127 --> 00:18:44.467
"You hear about immigrant children

00:18:44.467 --> 00:18:46.417
"dying at the border, but you don't hear

00:18:46.417 --> 00:18:50.270
"that these are indigenous
children dying at the border."

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:53.290
And this is from a
video I did last October

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:58.290
for Indigenous Peoples'
Day where we explored,

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:00.670
why are the majority of the children

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:02.820
dying at the border are indigenous?

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:04.370
Why aren't we asking this question?

00:19:04.370 --> 00:19:07.135
The headlines buried a lot.

00:19:07.135 --> 00:19:10.310
These are the indigenous
Guatemalan children

00:19:10.310 --> 00:19:14.560
that have passed away at the border.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:19.430
And there's another child
that died after them,

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:24.403
or like died during the same
period but was reported later.

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:26.370
It was a Salvadoran girl.

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:28.020
I don't think she was indigenous,

00:19:33.144 --> 00:19:36.230
but these children weren't
just indigenous Maya.

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:39.304
They belong to different Maya nations.

00:19:39.304 --> 00:19:43.043
Claudia was Maya Mam,
Jakelin was Maya Q'echi,

00:19:44.887 --> 00:19:48.020
Felipe was Chuj, Juan was Maya Ch'orti,

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:50.410
Wilmer was Ch'orti as well.

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:55.410
And Carlos was Achi and so I
never saw too much dwelling

00:19:57.820 --> 00:20:01.240
on their identity as indigenous peoples.

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:02.810
They were called migrant children,

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:05.130
at best they were called
indigenous Guatemalan children

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:06.080
but they were mostly just called

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:08.550
Guatemala migrant
children in the headlines.

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:13.550
And I felt that that was
leaving a lot out of the story.

00:20:16.127 --> 00:20:21.127
And so then I looked up what
about the indigenous identity

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:27.470
of these children if formed
why they had to migrate?

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:30.330
And if you look at the numbers

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:33.010
of inequality in Guatemala,
the malnutrition rate

00:20:33.010 --> 00:20:34.460
is high across the whole country.

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:36.290
It's even higher up to 70%

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:41.013
in more heavily indigenous
populated areas.

00:20:42.130 --> 00:20:44.860
79% of the indigenous people
in Guatemala live in poverty,

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:46.523
40% in extreme poverty.

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:52.023
So what you're seeing here is,

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:58.010
indigenous Guatemalans facing
more institutional neglect

00:20:58.010 --> 00:20:59.560
than non-indigenous Guatemalans

00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:01.860
and then you sort of start connecting,

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:06.860
so like they're also the
ones disproportionately dying

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:09.270
at the border, as well as in Guatemala.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:13.350
And they're also being
displaced from ancestral lands

00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:16.210
by palm oil plantations as
was the case of Jakelin Caal.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:22.070
There's also the issue
of language injustice.

00:21:22.070 --> 00:21:23.320
These are the languages,

00:21:23.320 --> 00:21:25.170
officially recognizing
languages in Guatemala.

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:26.900
There may be more, these are just the ones

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:28.840
that the government recognizes.

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:33.220
The vast majority of
them are Maya languages.

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:36.100
One is Garífuna another
one I think is Xinca

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:37.550
that are not Maya,

00:21:37.550 --> 00:21:39.570
but these are the indigenous
languages of Guatemala.

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:41.697
There's like 22 to 23 of them,

00:21:41.697 --> 00:21:44.280
depending on what count you use.

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.790
And this was a huge part of the story.

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:49.190
And eventually people
started reporting on this,

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:51.500
but I remember when it was breaking

00:21:51.500 --> 00:21:54.210
I was just kind of like, do
we know where she's from?

00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:55.830
Do we know if it's from an indigenous area

00:21:55.830 --> 00:22:00.640
and for Jakelin Caal's story,
this had a lot to do with it.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:03.660
In fact, three Maya
languages, not just one,

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:06.360
three mile languages,
Mopan, K'che and Q'anjob'al,

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:09.400
are some of the most used languages

00:22:09.400 --> 00:22:11.380
in immigration court in the top 25.

00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:16.373
The problem is there is not
enough interpreters and so,

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:20.830
they're probably not getting
the same kind of attention

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:25.820
articulating their migration
and getting the same kind

00:22:25.820 --> 00:22:27.770
of attention that Spanish speakers get.

00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:33.800
And statistics say that
speaking indigenous language

00:22:35.460 --> 00:22:38.740
means that you'll likelier
to be denied medical care.

00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:42.350
People who speak Spanish or English,

00:22:42.350 --> 00:22:43.610
already get denied medical care

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:45.110
but there's this another layer

00:22:45.960 --> 00:22:47.350
with people who speak indigenous languages

00:22:47.350 --> 00:22:51.020
that they're not listened
to, and a lot of the children

00:22:51.020 --> 00:22:56.020
who died in ICE custody were indigenous,

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:57.800
may have spoken indigenous language

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:02.800
or they were imprisoned with,
as in Jakelin Caal's case,

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:06.350
father who spoke his native language

00:23:06.350 --> 00:23:10.370
which is a Mayan language
and didn't receive

00:23:10.370 --> 00:23:11.530
the same kind of attention.

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:14.300
Since people may not have understood.

00:23:14.300 --> 00:23:16.730
And the language barrier that has to do

00:23:16.730 --> 00:23:18.320
with their identity is huge.

00:23:18.320 --> 00:23:19.930
It's a huge part of their story.

00:23:19.930 --> 00:23:23.700
It's a huge part of why they're leaving.

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:25.630
What the moment first
place not even to say

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:29.593
why they're more likely
to die in ICE custody.

00:23:30.898 --> 00:23:33.474
And then there was more deaths.

00:23:33.474 --> 00:23:35.250
There was more deaths.

00:23:35.250 --> 00:23:37.710
This is the one that really shook me.

00:23:37.710 --> 00:23:40.290
This really shook me because
there were Salvadoran

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:45.200
and it was a death of Oscar
Alberto Martinez Ramirez

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:48.360
and his daughter by Valeria,
I'm not gonna show the photo.

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:50.463
I think a lot of us remember the photo,

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:56.270
they were trying to get
to Brownsville, Texas,

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:59.000
I think over the Rio
Grande and they drowned.

00:23:59.000 --> 00:24:01.070
On that Rio Grande I'm not quite sure

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:05.550
but they drowned in a
river and their dead bodies

00:24:07.140 --> 00:24:10.823
were on display on every network.

00:24:12.578 --> 00:24:16.220
This started such a heated newsroom debate

00:24:17.170 --> 00:24:20.420
or whether should we show it?

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:23.750
Should anyone show it just
because CNN is doing it?

00:24:23.750 --> 00:24:24.693
Should we do it?

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:29.340
And that photo again, captivated everyone.

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:31.540
There was a ton of art about it

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:36.540
but a lot of people don't
remember who these people were.

00:24:38.850 --> 00:24:40.880
We can make a lot of assumptions

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:45.140
that people are fleeing El Salvador

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:47.160
in this case because of violence.

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:51.310
It has this image of violence,
the gangs, homicide, rape,

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:54.460
often one of the most dangerous
countries in the world.

00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:56.300
I think people already
sort of have a narrative

00:24:56.300 --> 00:24:58.470
as to why people are leaving El Salvador.

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:01.270
And poverty is one of them, but I feel

00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:06.270
that poverty in the case of Oscar Martinez

00:25:06.730 --> 00:25:10.933
was a bigger factor than violence.

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:16.200
He worked at Papa John's,
he made $350 a month.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:18.160
Between him and his partner,

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:20.260
they couldn't really make ends meet

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:23.433
and that was their motivation to migrate.

00:25:28.930 --> 00:25:30.910
We talk a lot about the
military intervention

00:25:30.910 --> 00:25:33.150
in this country, but so
much of the intervention

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:37.130
is sort of invisible and it's economic.

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:38.570
It's not just soldiers.

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:42.193
It's not just arms,
it's not just security.

00:25:44.890 --> 00:25:46.810
What is the economy like in El Salvador

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:50.960
that is making people
like Oscar Martinez flee.

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:53.910
Here's just a little snapshot
of the economy in El Salvador

00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:59.220
incredibly neoliberalised post to war.

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:01.650
Remember the Northern Triangle started off

00:26:01.650 --> 00:26:04.730
as a economic zone sort of.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:07.670
And this was a region

00:26:07.670 --> 00:26:12.670
that was very much
economically affected by war

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:18.100
and made her vulnerable
for free trade to come in

00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:19.870
and rebuild the region.

00:26:19.870 --> 00:26:23.992
And it went very extreme with this.

00:26:23.992 --> 00:26:27.310
Post-war we had 20 years of a right wing

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:30.260
sort of oligarchy running things

00:26:30.260 --> 00:26:31.900
and they never really
stopped running things

00:26:31.900 --> 00:26:36.900
but we had 10 years of leftist FMLN.

00:26:37.290 --> 00:26:42.290
And so we had neoliberal
reforms that privatized

00:26:42.700 --> 00:26:44.890
telecommunications, banks, electricity,

00:26:44.890 --> 00:26:47.120
and what this did was it made the cost

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:48.770
of especially electricity
and telecommunications

00:26:48.770 --> 00:26:52.433
double or triple for the population,

00:26:54.910 --> 00:26:56.740
and the economies dollarized.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:26:58.030
That's something that
it's very understated.

00:26:58.030 --> 00:27:02.213
It's about El Salvador has been
a dollar economy since 2001.

00:27:05.114 --> 00:27:08.110
And so it has increasingly
made the country

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:11.643
economically harder to live in.

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:17.373
So what has made what Guatemala,

00:27:18.456 --> 00:27:22.327
Honduras and El Salvador so
unlivable before migrants.

00:27:27.120 --> 00:27:28.903
Sorry, I was looking at a comment.

00:27:32.740 --> 00:27:33.823
So lemme see.

00:27:36.087 --> 00:27:41.087
I think it's very much a very
big narrative that gangs.

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:44.320
I'm not gonna dwell on gangs.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:48.530
I feel that often when I'm
asked to talk about El Salvador

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:52.610
or I'm invited to the table
or the editorial table

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:55.060
in the past, was because
Trump says something

00:27:55.060 --> 00:27:56.790
about MS-13, he calls him bed on brace.

00:27:56.790 --> 00:27:59.270
He calls him animals, and
now we have to respond to it.

00:27:59.270 --> 00:28:01.473
And we have to talk about it.

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:06.820
Long story short, MS-13
was born in the USA

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:12.760
and it has become our
prevailing image of ourselves.

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:18.970
And there's so much
behind the gang at play

00:28:20.950 --> 00:28:24.720
that works against people
trying to make a life

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:27.463
in Central America, trying to
survive in Central America.

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:31.170
One of those huge elephants in the room

00:28:31.170 --> 00:28:34.070
is U.S intervention in Central America.

00:28:34.070 --> 00:28:36.580
And you can't talk about the gang problem

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:38.410
without U.S intervention Central America

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:41.523
because a lot of those kids
were more refugees for one.

00:28:43.810 --> 00:28:47.150
A lot of those people
came here fleeing war

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:50.620
and then came into Ronald Reagan's America

00:28:50.620 --> 00:28:53.070
with no safety net and
then got mass deported

00:28:53.070 --> 00:28:57.440
in the nineties under the
Clinton administration,

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:59.803
once they went harder on immigration.

00:29:06.370 --> 00:29:09.090
The U.S involvement in Central
America goes very far back.

00:29:09.090 --> 00:29:11.550
Here's a moment from February, 2019,

00:29:11.550 --> 00:29:16.130
where representative Ilhan
Omar, grilled Elliott Abrams

00:29:16.130 --> 00:29:18.453
on the Trump's Envoy Venezuela.

00:29:19.330 --> 00:29:22.450
Elliot Abrams oversaw a
lot of what was going on

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:23.840
in El Salvador at the time.

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:28.840
And so she used his history
to make a case against him

00:29:30.610 --> 00:29:32.143
not involved in Venezuela.

00:29:33.420 --> 00:29:36.337
And so, in that hearing she says,

00:29:36.337 --> 00:29:38.307
"In that hearing you dismissed

00:29:38.307 --> 00:29:42.127
"as communist propaganda report
about the massacre El Mozote

00:29:42.127 --> 00:29:45.577
"in which 800 civilians

00:29:45.577 --> 00:29:47.367
"were brutally murdered by U.S troops.

00:29:47.367 --> 00:29:50.346
"That including children
as young as two years old."

00:29:50.346 --> 00:29:54.154
El Mozote is the biggest scar
of the Salvadoran civil war.

00:29:54.154 --> 00:29:56.560
My family who fled in the early eighties

00:29:56.560 --> 00:29:58.310
doesn't really know a lot about it.

00:30:00.524 --> 00:30:04.927
It still evokes a lot of
(speaks in foreign language),

00:30:07.720 --> 00:30:11.860
a lot of just like feelings
for Salvadoran diaspora

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:14.720
because we haven't really
seen a whole lot of justice.

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:17.070
A whole village was scorched earth tactic,

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:22.070
just disappeared, burned,
children murdered, women murdered.

00:30:24.260 --> 00:30:27.280
I went to El Mozote 10 years ago

00:30:27.280 --> 00:30:31.720
and 20 or 30 years after the
massacre, there's still blood

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:34.563
on some of the bricks
that make the new church.

00:30:37.810 --> 00:30:40.980
Because original one was
breakdown with people inside.

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:45.980
And so Ilhan Omar somehow broke through

00:30:46.940 --> 00:30:49.910
the usual U.S amnesia of Central America.

00:30:49.910 --> 00:30:53.480
And wouldn't straight come to the juggler

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:58.480
and not only grilled Elliott
Abrams on, it's on a blog also

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:02.140
about the Ixil indigenous Maya massacre

00:31:02.140 --> 00:31:03.620
that happening with Guatemala

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:05.467
and the country war in Nicaragua.

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:13.110
And interest Googling El
Mozote hit a historical high.

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:17.957
It measures it here from one to a hundred,

00:31:17.957 --> 00:31:19.870
but a hundred means just like the peak

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:21.860
of what that term has been searched.

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:24.583
And that happened in February, 2019.

00:31:31.906 --> 00:31:33.739
And it was a big deal.

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:38.340
It brought back into the
collective consciousness

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:40.550
of the U.S a rare moment that like,

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:43.750
hey, Central American
refugees is not a problem

00:31:43.750 --> 00:31:45.800
that started with a Trump administration.

00:31:47.000 --> 00:31:49.290
Here's a snapshot of
some of the U.S military

00:31:49.290 --> 00:31:54.290
involvement in the region
by far not exhausted

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:56.753
