where I got a lot of my
following as early journalist,

00:47:22.940 --> 00:47:27.437
and my family also
helps ground me as well,

00:47:27.437 --> 00:47:30.023
they're part of my storytelling.

00:47:30.023 --> 00:47:33.320
Part of the reason I like to
involve them in my storytelling

00:47:33.320 --> 00:47:35.150
and it's kinda like, hey,
I'm working on the story,

00:47:35.150 --> 00:47:37.252
what do you think from your perspective?

00:47:37.252 --> 00:47:38.438
I wanna tell the story about the war

00:47:38.438 --> 00:47:43.210
but I have these facts, does
it line up with what you saw?

00:47:43.210 --> 00:47:45.710
Does it or not, and we
have a discussion about it.

00:47:49.387 --> 00:47:52.750
And sometimes those things
are too heavy to get into,

00:47:52.750 --> 00:47:55.970
because it can be very triggering
for folks in our community

00:47:55.970 --> 00:47:57.120
and even just sharing news

00:47:57.120 --> 00:47:58.920
and expecting my community to share it,

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:00.670
I know I'm exposing them to trauma.

00:48:02.200 --> 00:48:03.630
I think mental health resources,

00:48:03.630 --> 00:48:05.850
I'm lucky that as a full-time journalist

00:48:05.850 --> 00:48:08.245
that I have access to
mental health resources

00:48:08.245 --> 00:48:11.440
and every newsroom should be
'cause of the vicarious trauma.

00:48:11.440 --> 00:48:13.290
But on top of that being Central American,

00:48:13.290 --> 00:48:16.190
I think there is a lot
of work around trauma

00:48:16.190 --> 00:48:17.290
that we need to do.

00:48:17.290 --> 00:48:20.573
I encourage the reading of Martin-Baro,

00:48:22.990 --> 00:48:25.750
I think it's called
"Liberation Psychology"

00:48:25.750 --> 00:48:27.740
which specifically
addresses our war trauma.

00:48:27.740 --> 00:48:29.740
That informs a lot of our other traumas.

00:48:30.870 --> 00:48:34.500
Mental health and friends, yeah.

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:36.843
- I have a follow up question to that,

00:48:38.260 --> 00:48:43.060
growing up in a similar way
I very much recognize now

00:48:43.060 --> 00:48:46.403
that I grew up in both the
culture of trauma and silence.

00:48:47.840 --> 00:48:52.840
Just not knowing why the
heaviness was in the room

00:48:52.850 --> 00:48:55.090
or why things were the way they were.

00:48:55.090 --> 00:48:58.193
Partly because the stories
weren't being told,

00:48:59.538 --> 00:49:04.538
how do you get to that place
to even ask the questions

00:49:05.750 --> 00:49:06.760
of your family members?

00:49:06.760 --> 00:49:08.130
What is your story?

00:49:08.130 --> 00:49:10.910
- All right, I've been very
blessed to have interviewed

00:49:10.910 --> 00:49:12.830
two Holocaust survivors in my life.

00:49:12.830 --> 00:49:16.477
And they've informed how,

00:49:16.477 --> 00:49:19.690
the U.S doesn't have a good
framework to talk about war

00:49:19.690 --> 00:49:21.477
and trauma often like the world war II

00:49:21.477 --> 00:49:24.430
and the Holocaust is often the most

00:49:24.430 --> 00:49:26.990
I think we talk about fascism
and its effects on people.

00:49:26.990 --> 00:49:30.690
And so I drew a lot as a young person

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:34.670
from that and in interviewing
Holocaust survivors

00:49:34.670 --> 00:49:37.020
as both a young person in my community,

00:49:37.020 --> 00:49:39.720
but also as a journalist,

00:49:39.720 --> 00:49:43.900
one person told me that for
a lot of Holocaust survivors,

00:49:43.900 --> 00:49:45.580
their parents are two types.

00:49:45.580 --> 00:49:46.950
And I'm not comparing
the Salvadoran civil war

00:49:46.950 --> 00:49:49.500
to Holocaust, but an atrocity right?

00:49:49.500 --> 00:49:51.290
Some parents are completely silenced.

00:49:51.290 --> 00:49:53.820
They wanna forget, they wanna
move on because of the trauma.

00:49:53.820 --> 00:49:58.120
And some parents never
stopped talking about it.

00:49:58.120 --> 00:50:00.170
They never wanna forget, so the overshare,

00:50:00.170 --> 00:50:02.690
they may be overshare too much.

00:50:02.690 --> 00:50:04.660
They may not have access to therapy.

00:50:04.660 --> 00:50:06.720
So maybe they're telling
the family stories

00:50:06.720 --> 00:50:07.553
as the only thing they can

00:50:07.553 --> 00:50:09.710
and I was clearly part of that group.

00:50:09.710 --> 00:50:12.240
Where I was hearing war
stories at like eight or nine

00:50:12.240 --> 00:50:15.370
and being like I had to, my mom telling me

00:50:15.370 --> 00:50:19.020
that she had to identify her
her brother in a mass grave

00:50:19.020 --> 00:50:20.020
'cause he was in gallia

00:50:20.020 --> 00:50:21.360
and he was killed by the death squads.

00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:24.840
And so I was exposed to
these stories early on

00:50:24.840 --> 00:50:28.370
and I feel like it's blessing
in disguise because yes,

00:50:28.370 --> 00:50:31.770
it's a lot of burdens on
trauma but I was also given

00:50:31.770 --> 00:50:34.880
the tools to be able to tell
these stories, to seek them out

00:50:34.880 --> 00:50:36.730
and to have these
uncomfortable conversations.

00:50:36.730 --> 00:50:38.930
And for my parents that
had given me clarity

00:50:38.930 --> 00:50:42.020
my parents migrated in their
late twenties, early thirties.

00:50:42.020 --> 00:50:45.240
So around my age so they were
also very mentally astute

00:50:45.240 --> 00:50:46.600
as to what was going on around them.

00:50:46.600 --> 00:50:48.540
They saw their society
crumble around them.

00:50:48.540 --> 00:50:51.910
So I was very lucky that I
had parents who were open.

00:50:51.910 --> 00:50:55.400
But if you don't, I say, learn about it.

00:50:55.400 --> 00:50:56.860
And once your parents find that,

00:50:56.860 --> 00:50:57.740
'cause this is what I also did,

00:50:57.740 --> 00:50:59.570
once I did parents saw that
I was learning about it,

00:50:59.570 --> 00:51:01.320
that I was trying to
understand the reality.

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:02.973
They also opened up even more.

00:51:04.548 --> 00:51:07.490
That's been my journey with that.

00:51:07.490 --> 00:51:10.960
- Yeah, I don't remember
when but I remember seeing

00:51:10.960 --> 00:51:13.750
your Twitter and you sharing
something about like,

00:51:13.750 --> 00:51:17.040
oh, Salvi parents, will
just casually throw out

00:51:17.040 --> 00:51:21.120
or trauma and definitely in a really sick

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:24.090
and funny way felt rang true to me.

00:51:24.090 --> 00:51:26.160
So speaking about Twitter and media,

00:51:26.160 --> 00:51:29.720
we're gonna go to Emily Arman's question,

00:51:29.720 --> 00:51:34.097
who says, "Thank you for your
top notch Twitter content

00:51:34.097 --> 00:51:36.070
"and your journalism."

00:51:36.070 --> 00:51:38.270
As a Central American scholar,

00:51:38.270 --> 00:51:41.920
she can't express how much it
has helped her own research

00:51:41.920 --> 00:51:43.447
and organizing work.

00:51:43.447 --> 00:51:46.727
"I'm wondering if you can
talk a bit about how the media

00:51:46.727 --> 00:51:50.147
"that sometimes tries to
lend sympathy towards those,

00:51:50.147 --> 00:51:54.257
"this place from Central
America often infantilizes

00:51:54.257 --> 00:51:56.187
"the individuals being targeted

00:51:56.187 --> 00:51:58.757
"by state violence early children.

00:51:58.757 --> 00:52:03.357
"How can journalism academia
organization dissolve the idea

00:52:03.357 --> 00:52:07.137
"that an individual community
is deserving of rights,

00:52:07.137 --> 00:52:10.597
"respect, often retroactive human decency

00:52:10.597 --> 00:52:14.367
"and garner public support
for their agency and migration

00:52:14.367 --> 00:52:18.217
"without labeling unwavering innocence

00:52:18.217 --> 00:52:21.287
"and passive victim hood
as a means of doing so?"

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:26.940
- Wow, that's a good question.

00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:27.860
That's something that I'm trying to figure

00:52:27.860 --> 00:52:30.023
out myself as well.

00:52:31.790 --> 00:52:36.790
I'm constantly tasked
with having to humanize

00:52:37.130 --> 00:52:39.969
Central Americans in a way that people

00:52:39.969 --> 00:52:42.880
in a news organization or people online

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:47.880
may not be familiar with and
I think part of that is just

00:52:51.610 --> 00:52:56.610
seeking out Central American
voices to speak for themselves

00:52:58.510 --> 00:53:00.570
and also getting Central
American journalism

00:53:00.570 --> 00:53:03.240
and professionals who can speak to it,

00:53:03.240 --> 00:53:07.720
not just professionals but
also have them in the mix too,

00:53:07.720 --> 00:53:11.450
because as much as it's about

00:53:11.450 --> 00:53:13.410
getting the right people in the voices.

00:53:13.410 --> 00:53:14.540
You're not gonna get those people

00:53:14.540 --> 00:53:16.490
unless you are part of their community,

00:53:17.510 --> 00:53:19.333
unless you're interacting with them.

00:53:20.540 --> 00:53:25.540
So partially is also getting
Central Americans in the room

00:53:26.507 --> 00:53:29.840
not just Salvador, is not just Mistasis,

00:53:29.840 --> 00:53:33.030
not just white Central
Americans, but getting people

00:53:33.030 --> 00:53:34.950
who know how to talk about this community

00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:39.880
who can go beyond the pass the
victimhood and the innocence

00:53:39.880 --> 00:53:42.073
and tell fuller pictures.

00:53:43.252 --> 00:53:45.205
I think it's kinda two-prong,

00:53:45.205 --> 00:53:48.390
It's getting the person
to ask the right questions

00:53:48.390 --> 00:53:50.900
to journalists and finding the people,

00:53:50.900 --> 00:53:55.507
and basically opening
pathways to black indigenous

00:53:56.750 --> 00:54:00.400
and journalists of color in the newsroom.

00:54:00.400 --> 00:54:03.630
I benefited so much from Al Jazeera

00:54:03.630 --> 00:54:06.730
being a middle Eastern focused newsroom

00:54:06.730 --> 00:54:10.530
with a lot of people of
color in the newsroom.

00:54:10.530 --> 00:54:14.410
And they talked about issues of war

00:54:14.410 --> 00:54:16.020
and all that stuff often,

00:54:16.020 --> 00:54:18.480
so I don't know what it's like being

00:54:18.480 --> 00:54:20.683
in an all white
institution and doing this.

00:54:21.820 --> 00:54:23.020
That's part of the
reason I went to Twitter

00:54:23.020 --> 00:54:24.830
to yell about it.

00:54:24.830 --> 00:54:28.550
I think if these institutions,
academia, organizing whatever

00:54:28.550 --> 00:54:31.570
aren't doing it for you
then create your own path

00:54:31.570 --> 00:54:32.670
yelling into the void,

00:54:32.670 --> 00:54:34.800
find people to have
this conversation with,

00:54:34.800 --> 00:54:37.410
because it's also a conversation
that Central Americans

00:54:37.410 --> 00:54:39.030
we should just be having
in the media and public.

00:54:39.030 --> 00:54:41.206
It's something that we should
be having with ourselves,

00:54:41.206 --> 00:54:42.940
that people got community.

00:54:42.940 --> 00:54:44.680
But yeah, that's a difficult question.

00:54:44.680 --> 00:54:46.920
I don't feel like I answered it too well

00:54:46.920 --> 00:54:49.423
but it's something that
I still struggle with.

00:54:51.150 --> 00:54:53.320
- I got a message separately from Emily

00:54:53.320 --> 00:54:56.470
saying that that was a
great answer, so thank you.

00:54:56.470 --> 00:54:59.690
As my follow-up to that,
about a year or two ago,

00:54:59.690 --> 00:55:03.370
we had a different
pointer fellow journalist

00:55:03.370 --> 00:55:07.730
who was American with no relational ties

00:55:07.730 --> 00:55:09.810
to the Central American region.

00:55:09.810 --> 00:55:13.400
Award-winning journalist in their coverage

00:55:13.400 --> 00:55:16.982
of gang life and some--

00:55:16.982 --> 00:55:20.770
And you mentioned earlier
how these are often

00:55:20.770 --> 00:55:22.110
how we're depicted.

00:55:22.110 --> 00:55:25.130
So it's either we're
victims or we're criminals.

00:55:25.130 --> 00:55:29.850
Why is responsible reporting specifically

00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:32.970
for journalists that are
looking into the area

00:55:32.970 --> 00:55:34.773
from an outsider gaze?

00:55:36.060 --> 00:55:38.480
- Can you repeat that last sentence?

00:55:38.480 --> 00:55:40.780
- What responsibilities do journalists

00:55:40.780 --> 00:55:42.950
have that are reporting in the region

00:55:42.950 --> 00:55:46.273
that are looking at it
from an outsider gaze?

00:55:49.420 --> 00:55:52.650
- Very simple, listen to
Central Americans right.

00:55:52.650 --> 00:55:54.690
That's been frustrating with me too

00:55:54.690 --> 00:55:57.680
because these are people in the industry

00:55:57.680 --> 00:56:00.900
that I've had to become
friends with and learn from.

00:56:00.900 --> 00:56:03.720
And these are the sort of
the gatekeepers at ministry,

00:56:03.720 --> 00:56:08.720
but it's this fine line of being
able to voice your critique

00:56:14.150 --> 00:56:15.720
but also not upsetting the gatekeepers.

00:56:15.720 --> 00:56:18.763
It sucks, it's in a lot of
industries, it's an academia.

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:25.661
I don't honestly concern
too much with those people

00:56:25.661 --> 00:56:29.810
all the time, obviously I have
the write to their gaze often

00:56:29.810 --> 00:56:34.780
and encounter that but
instead of putting my energy

00:56:34.780 --> 00:56:37.540
into correcting every white
person, every white journo

00:56:37.540 --> 00:56:39.363
who gets something wrong,

00:56:40.860 --> 00:56:42.660
that's energy that I could be putting

00:56:42.660 --> 00:56:45.990
into the Central American community

00:56:45.990 --> 00:56:50.990
mentoring other journalists,
elevating the voices of people

00:56:51.540 --> 00:56:54.823
who are talking about this
in an inappropriate way.

00:56:58.740 --> 00:57:01.970
It's jarring, that's part of the reason

00:57:01.970 --> 00:57:02.803
I came into this work.

00:57:02.803 --> 00:57:05.371
I don't really know if I
would've been in journalism,

00:57:05.371 --> 00:57:07.540
if it wasn't for 2014 and
me being just so pissed off

00:57:07.540 --> 00:57:09.790
at how mainly white
journalists were covering,

00:57:09.790 --> 00:57:12.020
like, oh my God, where did
they come from these migrants?

00:57:12.020 --> 00:57:13.220
Why are they so violent?

00:57:15.030 --> 00:57:17.170
And also like sometimes we
need to read these stuff

00:57:17.170 --> 00:57:22.030
to keep up to date, but I think
just always being relentless

00:57:22.030 --> 00:57:26.330
with the critical gaze and
don't be afraid to challenge

00:57:26.330 --> 00:57:29.760
these folks who are gatekeepers
in media and industry,

00:57:29.760 --> 00:57:32.220
don't feel free to challenge
me and people challenge me

00:57:32.220 --> 00:57:33.920
about the Northern Triangle stuff.

00:57:36.090 --> 00:57:37.490
Yeah.

00:57:37.490 --> 00:57:40.710
- Thank you, and I wish we had
time for all the questions,

00:57:40.710 --> 00:57:45.410
but one final one, I'm
gonna go to Jesselene Paz,

00:57:45.410 --> 00:57:47.310
who also happens to be the president

00:57:47.310 --> 00:57:50.020
of our Central Americans for Empowerment

00:57:50.020 --> 00:57:51.950
student group at Yale.

00:57:51.950 --> 00:57:54.107
She asked, "Do you have any advice

00:57:54.107 --> 00:57:57.177
"for young Central Americans
who want to tell stories

00:57:57.177 --> 00:58:00.647
"about Central America, but
not exclusively on trauma,

00:58:00.647 --> 00:58:03.740
"but also joy, family,
community, et cetera?"

00:58:03.740 --> 00:58:06.690
- Yes, write about those things,
create about those things.

00:58:06.690 --> 00:58:09.630
You may not think there is
an audience for those things,

00:58:09.630 --> 00:58:11.713
but there totally is.

00:58:14.590 --> 00:58:16.090
When I think about an account that focuses

00:58:16.090 --> 00:58:17.500
on a lot of like Central American joy

00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:20.050
I would say there's account
called Central American Beauty

00:58:20.050 --> 00:58:25.050
that just shows the beautiful
things of our isthmus,

00:58:25.200 --> 00:58:27.710
the vegetation, the
diversity of its people,

00:58:27.710 --> 00:58:30.090
the traditions, the richness of them too.

00:58:30.090 --> 00:58:31.890
Tell those stories.

00:58:31.890 --> 00:58:33.080
My only advice is to tell them

00:58:33.080 --> 00:58:37.010
and who cares if people
don't want to hear them,

00:58:37.010 --> 00:58:39.100
put them on your Instagram,
put them on Twitter,

00:58:39.100 --> 00:58:42.863
put them on medium and
then people will come.

00:58:45.283 --> 00:58:48.720
We don't need the validation so much.

00:58:48.720 --> 00:58:51.290
I don't get the validation from
my peers and the journalism.

00:58:51.290 --> 00:58:53.280
I get my validation from what my community

00:58:53.280 --> 00:58:54.113
says about my story.

00:58:54.113 --> 00:58:55.770
If they liked the story and reformed

00:58:55.770 --> 00:58:59.420
their worldview about us,
then that for me is a win.

00:58:59.420 --> 00:59:02.210
But I struggle, I wanna tell more stories

00:59:02.210 --> 00:59:04.520
about the good things that
happen in Central America too.

00:59:04.520 --> 00:59:05.690
I think the closest I got

00:59:05.690 --> 00:59:08.977
to it was Palestinian Salvadorians.

00:59:09.820 --> 00:59:13.320
I did a 10 minute history
explainer about that.

00:59:13.320 --> 00:59:16.460
It's include war obviously,
but it was a lot more

00:59:16.460 --> 00:59:19.290
about their cultural
traditions and just do it.

00:59:19.290 --> 00:59:20.883
Don't wait for an opportunity.

00:59:22.310 --> 00:59:24.090
- Thank you, and thank you Daniel,

00:59:24.090 --> 00:59:26.030
this has been such a joy to have you here.

00:59:26.030 --> 00:59:28.860
I wish that we had more than an hour.

00:59:28.860 --> 00:59:31.380
I'm sure our audience members
have lots more to ask.

00:59:31.380 --> 00:59:32.813
So please follow Daniel.

00:59:34.490 --> 00:59:38.080
Could you quickly mention
again your Twitter handle or--

00:59:38.080 --> 00:59:42.950
- If you search for me,
Daniel Alvarenga on Twitter

00:59:42.950 --> 00:59:45.810
and Instagram, I don't know
the first thing that comes up

00:59:45.810 --> 00:59:50.020
but my handle is, let
me write it in the chat.

00:59:50.020 --> 00:59:51.620
Let's see if that'll be helpful.

00:59:52.630 --> 00:59:55.360
My Twitter handle it's @_danalvarenga

01:00:00.740 --> 01:00:05.740
and Instagram is @salvi_prince.

01:00:06.230 --> 01:00:07.620
I've had that account for a while

01:00:07.620 --> 01:00:10.240
and it's just become a thing,
it's not super professional

01:00:10.240 --> 01:00:12.423
but it is what it is.

01:00:14.770 --> 01:00:16.730
And don't forget to watch and subscribe

01:00:16.730 --> 01:00:18.640
on Radar2020 if you wanna see more

01:00:18.640 --> 01:00:20.050
of my stories in the future.

01:00:20.050 --> 01:00:23.070
- Great, thank you and
to all those watching

01:00:23.070 --> 01:00:26.230
please follow other Central
American journalists

01:00:26.230 --> 01:00:28.607
and writers and scholars we're out here.

01:00:28.607 --> 01:00:31.340
So representation definitely matters.

01:00:31.340 --> 01:00:33.580
Finally, I wanna give a huge thanks

01:00:33.580 --> 01:00:36.830
to Yale College alumni Alfred Caldwell

01:00:36.830 --> 01:00:39.780
who organized all of this last year

01:00:39.780 --> 01:00:42.565
but unfortunately we
couldn't make it happen

01:00:42.565 --> 01:00:46.080
as the pandemic happened but here we are.

01:00:46.080 --> 01:00:50.010
So thank you for your work and
labor of love for your peers.

01:00:50.010 --> 01:00:51.900
And that's it.

01:00:51.900 --> 01:00:54.770
Thank you all and have a
wonderful rest of your day.

01:00:54.770 --> 01:00:56.070
- Thank you for having me.

